  "title": "Whom to invite",
  "sourceHash": "sha256:82c8dbeef97c67e65b49d14e8768b7d00fe1314e62383e97fe00bf3e1e7910a0",
  "outline": [
    {
      "heading": "Where participants come from",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "A leader usually has three sources: current clients with a similar request; their friends who have asked about you; the waiting list.",
          "origin": "extract"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "heading": "Selection",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Reviews of group coaching say it plainly: the group format is not for everyone, and participants are selected in advance.",
          "origin": "extract"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "heading": "Three criteria",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "Three criteria are enough for a first meeting.",
          "origin": "extract"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "heading": "Open or closed",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "In an open group, newcomers end up on an unequal footing.",
          "origin": "extract"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "heading": "A worked example",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "The leader has five people on her waiting list and two clients who asked about a group.",
          "origin": "extract"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "heading": "Not everyone at once",
      "points": [
        {
          "text": "The more precise the list, the better the chance that the meeting becomes a beginning, not a one-off experience.",
          "origin": "extract"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    }
  ],
